[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it holds three
problems. Two of them look the same to a reporter: a date with day and month swapped. The third does
not swap anything: the date moves by one day.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not known. Three problems, three tickets. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## How to tell them apart

- **`parser`**: the tenant writes the day first and the report came by app, email, or a
  `booking-v2` form. The shared parser reads the date month first, so day and month swap.
- **`hint`**: the tenant writes the day first and the report came through the `booking-v3` form.
  That form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says, and the form reads it
  day first. Same swap, different code.
- **`shift`**: the date is one day off, not swapped ("Lisbon got Tuesday, Toronto got Monday").
  A month-first parser cannot do that; a time zone can. The desk already read these as a one-day
  move (`desk-notes.json`). The desk counts them under their tenant's cause, so the table does
  too; they also have their own ticket, because the parser fix does not touch them.

The subject line tells none of this: the same subjects appear under all three. That is why merging
on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put them in one ticket. The
tenant, the channel and the body tell them apart. Intake's tag does not: it is a guess from the
browser.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A big ticket can be several bugs with one symptom. Before you mark it known, count by cause.
- Read the reports that do not fit. Five odd ones were a third bug.

